('127.394.d.8.D',3,'To build an AND gate using only NAND gates, you follow a NAND with:','["a resistor", "a capacitor", "an inverter made from a NAND", "a battery"]'::jsonb,2,'AND = NOT(NAND).','original'),
('127.394.d.8.D',4,'How many 2-input NAND gates are needed to make a 2-input OR gate?','["2", "5", "1", "3"]'::jsonb,3,'OR = NAND(NOT A, NOT B): two inverters plus one NAND.','original'),
('127.394.d.8.E',3,'In the design process, simplifying the logic expression comes before:','["drawing the final gate-level schematic", "listing the inputs", "defining the problem", "writing the specification"]'::jsonb,0,'Simplify, then draw.','original'),
('127.394.d.8.E',4,'Designers verify a simplified expression against the original truth table to:','["change the inputs", "confirm it still gives the same outputs", "add gates", "skip testing"]'::jsonb,1,'Simplification must preserve function.','original'),
('127.394.d.8.F',3,'Using only NAND gates in a design can reduce cost because the design needs:','["more wiring types", "extra power supplies", "fewer types of ICs", "bigger boards"]'::jsonb,2,'One chip type covers everything.','original'),
('127.394.d.8.F',4,'Building a circuit entirely from one gate type simplifies:','["sales", "law", "math", "inventory and layout"]'::jsonb,3,'Uniform parts are easier to stock and place.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.A',3,'A standard calculator digit display has how many lighted bars, not counting the decimal point?','["seven", "five", "ten", "nine"]'::jsonb,0,'Seven segments form digits.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.A',4,'To show the digit 1 on a seven-segment display, which segments are lit?','["a and g", "b and c", "a and d", "e and f"]'::jsonb,1,'The two right-side segments.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.B',3,'The two varieties of seven-segment displays are common anode and:','["common diode", "common ground only", "common cathode", "common relay"]'::jsonb,2,'Segments share either anode or cathode.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.B',4,'In a common-anode display, a segment lights when its cathode pin is driven:','["HIGH", "negative twice", "open", "LOW"]'::jsonb,3,'Current flows from the common anode through the segment to a LOW pin.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.C',3,'A BCD-to-seven-segment decoder converts a:','["4-bit binary code to segment signals", "clock to voltage", "pulse to current", "light to sound"]'::jsonb,0,'The decoder drives segments a through g.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.C',4,'In designing a BCD-to-seven-segment decoder, the number of input bits needed is:','["two", "four", "seven", "ten"]'::jsonb,1,'BCD digits 0-9 need four bits.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.D',3,'The hexadecimal digit F equals what decimal value?','["14", "16", "15", "10"]'::jsonb,2,'A-F represent 10-15.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.D',4,'The binary number 11010110 written in hexadecimal is:','["C6", "6D", "B6", "D6"]'::jsonb,3,'1101 = D, 0110 = 6.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.E',3,'An XOR gate outputs HIGH when:','["its inputs are different", "its inputs never change", "both inputs are LOW", "both inputs are the same"]'::jsonb,0,'Exclusive OR detects difference.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.E',4,'An XNOR gate is often used as a:','["memory", "single-bit equality detector", "power regulator", "clock"]'::jsonb,1,'XNOR is HIGH when inputs match.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.F',3,'In binary, 1 + 1 equals:','["01", "2", "10", "11"]'::jsonb,2,'Sum 0 with a carry of 1.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.F',4,'A full adder has inputs A, B, and:','["reset only", "clock", "ground only", "carry-in"]'::jsonb,3,'It adds three bits.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.G',3,'A multiplexer selects one of several inputs and sends it to:','["a single output", "a memory", "no output", "every output"]'::jsonb,0,'Many in, one out.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.G',4,'A 4-to-1 multiplexer needs how many select lines?','["one", "two", "four", "eight"]'::jsonb,1,'2 bits choose among 4 inputs.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.H',3,'A de-multiplexer sends one input to one of several:','["clocks", "resistors", "outputs", "power rails"]'::jsonb,2,'One in, many out.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.H',4,'Multiplexing a multi-digit display lights one digit at a time quickly so the display uses fewer:','["colors", "segments", "digits", "driver pins and less current"]'::jsonb,3,'Shared segment lines save pins.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.I',3,'The most common method for storing negative binary numbers is:','["two''s complement", "ten''s complement", "letter coding", "octal flipping"]'::jsonb,0,'Two''s complement simplifies arithmetic.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.I',4,'In 8-bit two''s complement, the leftmost bit indicates the number''s:','["color", "sign", "parity", "frequency"]'::jsonb,1,'1 means negative.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.J',3,'A programmable logic device (PLD) is a chip whose logic function is set by:','["its color", "its package", "programming it", "its price"]'::jsonb,2,'PLDs are configured by the user.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.J',4,'An example of a PLD family is the:','["resistor array", "power cord", "crystal", "GAL or CPLD"]'::jsonb,3,'GALs and CPLDs are programmable logic.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.K',3,'A PLD can replace several discrete logic chips, which usually reduces:','["board space", "reliability", "flexibility", "programmability"]'::jsonb,0,'One chip does the work of many.','original'),
('127.394.d.9.K',4,'A drawback of using a PLD instead of simple gates is that it:','["uses more gates", "needs programming tools", "cannot be changed", "has no pins"]'::jsonb,1,'You need software and a programmer.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.A',3,'A D flip-flop transfers the D input to its output on the:','["reset only", "falling resistance", "active clock edge", "power-up only"]'::jsonb,2,'Edge-triggered storage.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.A',4,'A transparent latch differs from a flip-flop because while enabled, its output:','["is always LOW", "never changes", "waits for the clock edge", "follows the input"]'::jsonb,3,'Latches are level-sensitive.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.B',3,'An asynchronous input, such as reset, affects the flip-flop:','["immediately, regardless of the clock", "only on the clock edge", "only on Friday", "never"]'::jsonb,0,'Async inputs override the clock.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.B',4,'Synchronous inputs such as D are recognized only:','["when unplugged", "at the clock edge", "with a reset", "at power-off"]'::jsonb,1,'They are sampled on the edge.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.C',3,'A circuit that remembers a button was pressed even after release uses a:','["diode", "fuse", "flip-flop", "resistor"]'::jsonb,2,'Memory element.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.C',4,'A switch debouncer made with an SR latch prevents:','["power loss", "excess heat", "display flicker", "multiple false pulses from contact bounce"]'::jsonb,3,'The latch settles on the first contact.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.D',3,'In an asynchronous (ripple) counter, each flip-flop is clocked by:','["the output of the previous stage", "the reset line", "the same common clock", "the power supply"]'::jsonb,0,'Changes ripple through the stages.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.D',4,'A 3-bit binary counter can count through how many states?','["six", "eight", "nine", "three"]'::jsonb,1,'2^3 = 8.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.E',3,'An up counter increases its count by one each time it receives a:','["power cycle", "reset", "clock pulse", "fuse"]'::jsonb,2,'Each pulse increments.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.E',4,'A down counter that starts at 5 (101) goes next to:','["7 (111)", "6 (110)", "0 (000)", "4 (100)"]'::jsonb,3,'Counts down by one.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.F',3,'In a synchronous counter, all flip-flops share the same:','["clock signal", "reset resistor", "power plug", "output"]'::jsonb,0,'They are clocked together.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.F',4,'A synchronous counter avoids ripple delay because all stages:','["skip clock edges", "change state at the same clock edge", "ignore the clock", "change one after another"]'::jsonb,1,'No cascaded delays.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.G',3,'A mod-10 counter counts from 0 up to:','["10", "15", "9", "8"]'::jsonb,2,'Ten states, 0-9.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.G',4,'To make a 4-bit counter restart after 9, detect the count 1010 and use it to drive the:','["output enable only", "power input", "clock input", "reset or clear input"]'::jsonb,3,'Decoding the invalid count clears the counter.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.H',3,'A state machine is a circuit that moves between a limited number of:','["defined states", "voltages only", "colors", "chips"]'::jsonb,0,'Finite states.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.H',4,'In a state diagram, the arrows between circles show:','["wire lengths", "transitions between states", "component costs", "power ratings"]'::jsonb,1,'Arrows are transitions.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.I',3,'A traffic light controller is an example of a device controlled by a:','["capacitor", "fuse", "state machine", "crystal"]'::jsonb,2,'Lights cycle through states.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.I',4,'A vending machine uses a state machine to track the amount of money:','["spent by others", "earned last year", "in the bank", "inserted so far"]'::jsonb,3,'State remembers credit.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.J',3,'A state machine can be implemented with flip-flops and:','["combinational logic", "only resistors", "only batteries", "only wires"]'::jsonb,0,'Flip-flops hold state; logic computes next state.','original'),
('127.394.d.10.J',4,'Besides discrete logic, a state machine can also be implemented in:','["a paper form", "a microcontroller or PLD", "a fuse box", "a ruler"]'::jsonb,1,'Programmable devices can model states.','original'),
('127.394.d.11.A',3,'In a flowchart, a diamond shape represents a:','["process", "print", "decision", "start"]'::jsonb,2,'Diamonds branch on yes/no.','original'),
('127.394.d.11.A',4,'Flowcharts help programmers because they show the program''s:','["voltage", "color", "price", "logic flow before coding"]'::jsonb,3,'Planning before coding.','original'),
('127.394.d.11.B',3,'A variable in a program is used to:','["store a value that can change", "produce power", "draw wires", "measure heat"]'::jsonb,0,'Variables hold data.','original'),
('127.394.d.11.B',4,'A loop in a microcontroller program is used to:','["end power", "repeat instructions", "erase memory", "reduce voltage"]'::jsonb,1,'Loops repeat code.','original'),
('127.394.d.11.C',3,'Which everyday product contains a microcontroller?','["A paper cup", "A wooden spoon", "A microwave oven", "A brick"]'::jsonb,2,'Appliances use microcontrollers.','original'),
('127.394.d.11.C',4,'A robot vacuum uses a microcontroller to:','["grow plants", "cook food", "print pages", "read sensors and control motors"]'::jsonb,3,'Sense and act.','original'),
