('127.396.d.6.A',3,'A robot speeds up from 0 to 2 meters per second in 4 seconds. Its average acceleration is:','["0.5 meters per second squared", "2 meters per second squared", "0.2 meters per second squared", "8 meters per second squared"]'::jsonb,0,'2 / 4 = 0.5.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.A',4,'Velocity differs from speed because velocity includes:','["price", "direction", "mass", "color"]'::jsonb,1,'Velocity is a vector.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.B',3,'A hinge joint that rotates about a single axis provides how many degrees of freedom?','["zero", "six", "one", "three"]'::jsonb,2,'One axis, one degree.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.B',4,'A robot arm that can position its gripper anywhere in 3D space and orient it in any direction needs at least how many degrees of freedom?','["three", "two", "four", "six"]'::jsonb,3,'Three for position, three for orientation.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.C',3,'Angular momentum of a spinning part depends on its moment of inertia and its:','["angular velocity", "length of wire", "color", "price"]'::jsonb,0,'L = I x omega.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.C',4,'If a spinning arm pulls its mass closer to the axis while angular momentum is conserved, its rotation speed:','["decreases", "increases", "stops", "reverses"]'::jsonb,1,'Smaller I means greater omega.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.D',3,'Impulse equals force multiplied by:','["distance", "volume", "time", "mass"]'::jsonb,2,'J = F x t.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.D',4,'Extending the time over which a robot gripper stops a falling part reduces the:','["part''s mass", "gripper color", "gravity", "force on the part"]'::jsonb,3,'Same impulse over longer time means less force.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.E',3,'A robot wheel spinning in place shows which kind of motion?','["rotational motion", "translational motion", "no motion", "oscillatory motion"]'::jsonb,0,'Spinning is rotation.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.E',4,'A pendulum-like swing of a robot''s tail is an example of:','["random heat", "oscillatory motion", "no motion", "straight-line motion"]'::jsonb,1,'Back-and-forth repeating motion.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.F',3,'An H-bridge circuit allows a microcontroller to control a DC motor''s:','["weight", "color", "direction and speed", "name"]'::jsonb,2,'H-bridges reverse polarity and support PWM.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.F',4,'A motor''s stall current is the current drawn when the shaft is:','["removed", "disconnected", "spinning freely", "blocked and not turning"]'::jsonb,3,'Stall draws maximum current.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.G',3,'A continuous-rotation servo is controlled to set its:','["speed and direction", "color", "shape", "weight"]'::jsonb,0,'The pulse sets speed, not position.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.G',4,'A servo holding a heavy load may draw more current and need:','["a smaller battery", "an adequate power supply", "no wiring", "less torque rating"]'::jsonb,1,'Loaded servos draw more current.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.H',3,'A light sensor reads 200 on white and 800 on black. A good threshold to separate them is about:','["0", "900", "500", "100"]'::jsonb,2,'The midpoint is (200 + 800) / 2.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.H',4,'If a sensor reading is above the threshold, the program''s if-statement can trigger an:','["error only", "color change", "power-off always", "action such as turning"]'::jsonb,3,'Thresholds drive decisions.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.I',3,'To turn a robot 90 degrees in place, the wheels must travel an arc length determined by the robot''s:','["track width", "color", "name", "logo"]'::jsonb,0,'Wheel separation sets the turning arc.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.I',4,'A robot must drive along the hypotenuse of a 3-by-4 foot right triangle. The straight-line distance is:','["3.5 feet", "5 feet", "12 feet", "7 feet"]'::jsonb,1,'Pythagorean theorem: 3-4-5.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.J',3,'An encoder reports 360 counts per revolution. After 720 counts, the wheel has turned:','["four revolutions", "half a revolution", "two revolutions", "one revolution"]'::jsonb,2,'720 / 360 = 2.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.J',4,'To drive exactly 24 inches with a wheel of 8-inch circumference, the robot needs how many wheel rotations?','["four", "two", "eight", "three"]'::jsonb,3,'24 / 8 = 3.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.K',3,'Path planning with geometry and several sensors lets a robot find a route around:','["obstacles", "batteries", "colors", "speeds"]'::jsonb,0,'Planning avoids obstacles.','original'),
('127.396.d.6.K',4,'A robot uses ultrasonic and infrared sensors together to improve its path planning. This is called:','["sensor deletion", "sensor fusion", "battery balancing", "wheel locking"]'::jsonb,1,'Combining sensors improves reliability.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.A',3,'Syntax in programming means the:','["color of the code", "size of the screen", "rules for writing valid code", "speed of the CPU"]'::jsonb,2,'Syntax defines valid form.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.A',4,'A missing semicolon or bracket in code will most likely cause a:','["new feature", "power surge", "faster program", "syntax error"]'::jsonb,3,'The compiler cannot parse it.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.B',3,'Comments in a program help others to:','["understand what the code does", "delete it", "run it faster", "hide it"]'::jsonb,0,'Comments explain intent.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.B',4,'Good documentation for a robot program includes comments and a:','["list of jokes", "description of the sensors and logic", "sales pitch", "team photo"]'::jsonb,1,'Documentation aids maintenance.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.C',3,'An if-else structure lets a program:','["delete variables", "reduce voltage", "choose between actions based on a condition", "repeat forever"]'::jsonb,2,'Conditionals select paths.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.C',4,'Boolean logic in a program uses values that are either:','["big or small", "red or blue", "hot or cold", "true or false"]'::jsonb,3,'Boolean means two values.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.D',3,'Pseudocode is a way to describe an algorithm using:','["plain language steps", "wire colors", "machine code", "schematic symbols"]'::jsonb,0,'It is code-like English.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.D',4,'In a flowchart, an oval is used for:','["sensors", "start and end", "decisions", "motors"]'::jsonb,1,'Terminators are ovals.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.E',3,'An algorithm that stops the robot if the distance sensor reads under 10 centimeters uses a:','["loop counter only", "comment", "conditional statement", "header"]'::jsonb,2,'It tests a condition.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.E',4,'In pseudocode, IF obstacle detected THEN turn right is an example of:','["a variable", "a sensor", "a comment", "a selection structure"]'::jsonb,3,'Selection chooses an action.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.F',3,'To repeat an action exactly 5 times, a programmer uses a:','["counting loop", "header", "constant", "comment"]'::jsonb,0,'A for loop counts iterations.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.F',4,'An infinite loop in a robot''s main program is often used so the robot:','["skips inputs", "keeps running and checking sensors", "never starts", "turns off immediately"]'::jsonb,1,'Main loops run continuously.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.G',3,'Storing a sensor reading in a variable lets the program:','["stop the robot", "change the battery", "use and compare the value later", "erase the sensor"]'::jsonb,2,'Variables hold readings.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.G',4,'Feedback in code means using sensor values to adjust:','["comments", "screen colors", "file names", "outputs such as motor power"]'::jsonb,3,'Sensor data drives outputs.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.H',3,'A command that sets a motor''s power to 50 percent is an:','["output command", "syntax error", "input command", "comment"]'::jsonb,0,'It drives an actuator.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.H',4,'A variable named speed can store the motor power so it can be:','["never edited", "reused and changed in the program", "only printed", "deleted by hardware"]'::jsonb,1,'Variables are changeable.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.I',3,'A switch (case) structure is useful when a program must choose among:','["two colors only", "no options", "many possible actions", "hardware parts"]'::jsonb,2,'Switch handles multiple cases.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.I',4,'A goto or jump command changes the program''s:','["motor", "sensor", "voltage", "next instruction to execute"]'::jsonb,3,'Jumps redirect flow.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.J',3,'A function in a robot program is a reusable block that:','["performs a specific task", "stores sensors", "draws wires", "turns off power"]'::jsonb,0,'Functions encapsulate tasks.','original'),
('127.396.d.7.J',4,'Using a function for turning left 90 degrees avoids repeating:','["the battery", "the same code in many places", "the sensor", "the motor"]'::jsonb,1,'Reuse reduces errors.','original'),
('127.396.d.8.A',3,'Robotic arm links connected by joints form a:','["logic gate", "voltage divider", "kinematic chain", "power supply"]'::jsonb,2,'Links and joints form a chain.','original'),
('127.396.d.8.A',4,'The space a robot arm can reach is called its:','["power curve", "battery map", "shipping zone", "reach envelope"]'::jsonb,3,'Envelope = reachable workspace.','original'),
('127.396.d.8.B',3,'A joint motor produces half a newton-meter of torque through an ideal 10:1 gearbox. The output torque is:','["5 newton-meters", "0.05 newton-meters", "10.5 newton-meters", "0.5 newton-meters"]'::jsonb,0,'0.5 x 10 = 5.','original'),
('127.396.d.8.B',4,'Increasing a gear reduction usually trades away output:','["strength", "speed", "torque", "force"]'::jsonb,1,'More reduction means slower output.','original'),
('127.396.d.8.C',3,'A four-bar linkage in a gripper helps keep the fingers:','["cold", "upside down", "parallel as they close", "spinning"]'::jsonb,2,'Parallel linkage keeps jaws aligned.','original'),
('127.396.d.8.C',4,'A worm gear in a gripper actuator is often chosen because it:','["needs no motor", "spins freely", "has no friction", "resists back-driving and holds its position"]'::jsonb,3,'Worm drives self-lock.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.A',3,'The step where a team compares possible designs against the requirements is:','["evaluating alternatives", "shipping", "painting", "retiring"]'::jsonb,0,'Alternatives are compared.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Career Development' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
insert into public.question_bank (teks_code, teks_item_id, subject, grade_num, course, difficulty, qtype, prompt, choices, answer_idx, explanation, source, status, gen_batch)
select distinct on (v.prompt) v.code, i.id, d.subject,
       case when i.grade_level = 'K' then 0 when i.grade_level ~ '^[0-9]+' then substring(i.grade_level from '^[0-9]+')::smallint end,
       case when i.grade_level is null then i.grade_course_title end,
       v.diff, 'mc', v.prompt, v.choices, v.ans, v.expl, v.src, 'checked', 'cte_58_v1'
from (values
('127.396.d.9.A',4,'During the design process, requirements and constraints are written:','["after delivering the robot", "before choosing a design", "after the competition", "never"]'::jsonb,1,'They guide the choice.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.B',3,'A battery that can only supply 10 amps limits a design with motors that need 15 amps. This is a:','["size reward", "color constraint", "power constraint", "name constraint"]'::jsonb,2,'Supply limits the design.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.B',4,'Making design choices using test data and calculations is:','["guesswork", "luck", "imitation", "evidence-based reasoning"]'::jsonb,3,'Evidence supports decisions.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.C',3,'A formal test plan states the procedure, the measurements, and the:','["pass or fail criteria", "team jokes", "lunch plans", "logo ideas"]'::jsonb,0,'Criteria define success.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.C',4,'After analyzing test results, the next step in reiteration is to:','["stop the project", "revise the design and test again", "remove the data", "skip testing"]'::jsonb,1,'Iterate to improve.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.D',3,'To defend a design decision to judges, a team should present:','["guesses", "opinions only", "data and reasoning", "jokes"]'::jsonb,2,'Evidence persuades.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.D',4,'When two designs score equally in a decision matrix, the team might:','["flip a coin only", "pick randomly", "ignore the matrix", "add or reweight criteria and compare again"]'::jsonb,3,'Refine the criteria.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.E',3,'A control chart tracks a measurement over time to spot:','["variation or drift in quality", "lunch cost", "paint color", "team size"]'::jsonb,0,'Control charts detect process changes.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.E',4,'If 12 of 100 produced arms are out of tolerance, the defect rate is:','["100 percent", "12 percent", "88 percent", "1.2 percent"]'::jsonb,1,'12 / 100.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.F',3,'Six Sigma is a method for improving quality by reducing:','["profits", "employees", "defects and variation", "customers"]'::jsonb,2,'The goal is near-zero defects.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.F',4,'Six Sigma''s DMAIC stands for define, measure, analyze, improve, and:','["create", "cancel", "copy", "control"]'::jsonb,3,'The final phase is control.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.G',3,'In a robot engineering notebook, test data should be recorded with the:','["date, conditions, and results", "team''s gossip", "random doodles", "menus"]'::jsonb,0,'Context makes data useful.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.G',4,'A well-kept notebook can later help a team to:','["avoid reports", "reproduce and defend design decisions", "replace testing", "hide errors"]'::jsonb,1,'Records support defense and repeatability.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.H',3,'On a robot wiring schematic, a line between the battery and the motor driver shows a:','["gear mesh", "software comment", "electrical connection", "paint line"]'::jsonb,2,'Lines are wires.','original'),
('127.396.d.9.H',4,'Before powering a robot built from a schematic, check polarity to avoid:','["shorter wires", "brighter lights", "extra speed", "damaging components"]'::jsonb,3,'Reverse polarity can destroy parts.','original'),
('127.396.d.10.A',3,'A torque screwdriver is used to tighten fasteners to a:','["specified torque", "guess", "random tightness", "maximum force always"]'::jsonb,0,'It limits tightening.','original'),
('127.396.d.10.A',4,'Soldering iron tips and drill bits should be kept:','["dirty", "clean and in good condition", "bent", "loose in pockets"]'::jsonb,1,'Maintained tools are safer.','original'),
('127.396.d.10.B',3,'An oscilloscope helps analyze a robot''s motor-driver signals by showing the signal''s:','["price", "shape of the case", "waveform over time", "paint"]'::jsonb,2,'Scopes display waveforms.','original'),
('127.396.d.10.B',4,'A dial indicator is used to measure small amounts of:','["weight", "temperature", "voltage", "runout or movement"]'::jsonb,3,'Dial indicators detect small displacement.','original'),
('127.396.d.10.C',3,'Assigning a part number to each component of a robot lets a team:','["track and order parts accurately", "lose parts", "repaint parts", "hide parts"]'::jsonb,0,'Part numbers identify items.','original'),
('127.396.d.10.C',4,'A bill of materials for a robot lists each part''s quantity and:','["weather", "part number", "owner''s age", "paint recipe"]'::jsonb,1,'BOMs identify parts.','original'),
('127.396.d.10.D',3,'Physics-based robot simulators model a robot''s:','["marketing", "paint color", "motion and sensor behavior", "price"]'::jsonb,2,'They simulate dynamics.','original'),
('127.396.d.10.D',4,'To validate a simulation, compare its results with:','["opinions", "last year''s weather", "guesses", "measurements from the real robot"]'::jsonb,3,'Real data validates models.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.A',3,'The first step in designing a robot that sorts objects by color is to define its:','["requirements", "paint job", "logo", "carrying case"]'::jsonb,0,'Requirements drive design.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.A',4,'Sketching the layout of sensors, motors, and the controller before building is part of:','["marketing", "system design", "shipping", "accounting"]'::jsonb,1,'Layout planning.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.B',3,'Choosing sheet aluminum versus plastic for a chassis involves comparing strength, weight, and:','["season", "color names", "cost", "team size"]'::jsonb,2,'Materials trade-offs.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.B',4,'A laser cutter or CNC router is chosen when parts need:','["no precision", "random shapes", "hand painting", "accurate, repeatable cuts"]'::jsonb,3,'CNC gives precision.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.C',3,'A bump switch on a robot is used to detect:','["physical contact", "color", "temperature", "sound"]'::jsonb,0,'Touch sensor.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.C',4,'Mounting a distance sensor too low on a robot may cause it to:','["use less power", "read the floor or miss obstacles", "gain speed", "fix errors"]'::jsonb,1,'Placement affects readings.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.D',3,'While building the robot, a team should verify each subsystem works before:','["reading about it", "testing it", "integrating it with the rest", "painting it"]'::jsonb,2,'Test pieces before combining.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.D',4,'Securing wires with zip ties and strain relief prevents:','["sensor color", "extra speed", "lower voltage", "loose connections during motion"]'::jsonb,3,'Strain relief protects connectors.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.E',3,'Formal testing of a robot should be done using:','["a written procedure", "improvisation", "a guess", "random trials"]'::jsonb,0,'Procedures make tests repeatable.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.E',4,'Repeating a test several times and averaging results reduces the effect of:','["gravity", "random error", "design", "requirements"]'::jsonb,1,'Averaging smooths noise.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.F',3,'A robot sorts objects correctly only 80 percent of the time, but the goal is 95 percent. The team should:','["stop testing", "declare success", "refine the design and retest", "ignore the goal"]'::jsonb,2,'80 is below 95.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.F',4,'Refining the design for quality and safety could involve adding:','["loose wires", "sharp edges", "unprotected pinch points", "limit switches or emergency stops"]'::jsonb,3,'Safety features.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.G',3,'A final presentation of the robot should explain the design process and the:','["results of testing", "other robots'' secrets", "team''s snacks", "unrelated stories"]'::jsonb,0,'Results show success.','original'),
('127.396.d.11.G',4,'Using video of the robot in action during a presentation helps the audience:','["miss the details", "see the performance clearly", "lose interest", "skip questions"]'::jsonb,1,'Video demonstrates behavior.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
